1. What Node.js Is and Running JavaScript Outside the Browser

Node.js as a runtime: Node.js executes JavaScript code using the V8 engine directly on your operating system, independent of a web browser.
Browser vs. Server-side JavaScript: Browser JavaScript interacts with the DOM (document, window) to manipulate web pages. Server-side JavaScript interacts with the file system, network, and databases, lacking DOM APIs because there is no visual page to render.
Running a file != creating a server: Executing node index.js runs the script once from top to bottom and exits. A web server requires explicit code to listen continuously on a network port.
Checking versions: Run node -v and npm -v in the terminal to verify installations.

// index.js
const tasks = [
  { id: 1, title: "Learn JSX", completed: true },
  { id: 2, title: "Practise React state", completed: false },
  { id: 3, title: "Build a Node.js API", completed: false },
];

console.log("Hello from Node.js!");
console.log("Initial Tasks:", tasks);
Run with: node index.js

