2. npm, package.json and Project Scripts

npm init -y: Automatically creates a package.json file with default values, acting as the configuration blueprint for your project.
Dependencies vs. Development dependencies: dependencies are required to run the application (e.g., Express). devDependencies are only needed during development (e.g., testing frameworks, Prettier).
Manifests and Modules: package.json tracks project metadata. node_modules stores the downloaded package code. package-lock.json guarantees exact version matches across different machines.
npm install vs npm ci: install resolves and potentially updates dependencies. ci (Clean Install) strictly installs exact versions from package-lock.json, deleting the existing node_modules folder first.
npm scripts: Shortcuts defined in package.json to execute longer terminal commands (e.g., npm start).
Ignoring node_modules: Add node_modules/ to .gitignore so you don't commit massive, easily reproducible third-party code to Git.

// package.json (excerpt)
{
  "name": "task-api",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "node index.js"
  }
}
Run with: npm start

