5. Express and Your First API Route

Express: A minimal web framework for Node.js that simplifies routing, handling requests, and sending responses compared to raw Node.js HTTP modules.
Setup: Installed via npm install express.
req, res, res.json(): req holds incoming request data. res contains methods to send data back. res.json() safely converts a JavaScript object to a JSON string and sets the correct headers.
app.listen() & Ports: Binds the server to a specific port (like 3000) on localhost (your local machine), keeping the process alive to listen for incoming network traffic.
Separating app and server: Exporting the Express app without listening allows automated testing tools to make requests without binding to a live network port.

// src/app.js
import express from 'express';

const app = express();

app.get('/api/health', (req, res) => {
  res.status(200).json({ status: 'ok' });
});

export default app;

// src/server.js
import app from './app.js';

const PORT = 3000;
app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server running on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
});

