7. POST Routes, Request Bodies and Validation

express.json(): Built-in middleware that parses incoming requests with JSON payloads and populates req.body.
Content-Type: application/json: Required header from the client so Express knows to parse the body as JSON.
Server-Side Validation: Never trust client data. Even if React validates a form, a user can bypass the frontend using tools like Postman or cURL. The backend is the final line of defense.
ID Generation: The server is responsible for assigning unique IDs (e.g., using Date.now() or incrementing the highest existing ID).

// src/app.js (continued)
app.use(express.json()); // Global middleware for parsing JSON

app.post('/api/tasks', (req, res) => {
  const { title } = req.body;

  if (typeof title !== 'string' || title.trim() === '') {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: "Valid title is required" });
  }

  const newTask = {
    id: tasks.length > 0 ? Math.max(...tasks.map(t => t.id)) + 1 : 1,
    title: title.trim(),
    completed: false
  };

  tasks.push(newTask);
  res.status(201).json(newTask);
});

