9. Middleware and Consistent Error Handling

Middleware & Order: Functions that have access to req and res in the request-response cycle. They run sequentially top-to-bottom.
next(): Passes control to the next middleware or route handler. Without it, the request hangs.
Unknown Route Fallback: A middleware placed after all defined routes catches anything that didn't match and returns a 404.
Global Error Handler: An Express middleware with exactly four arguments (err, req, res, next). It catches unhandled exceptions to prevent the server from crashing and standardizes error responses without leaking stack traces to the client.

// src/app.js (continued - placed at the very bottom before export)

// 1. Request Logging Middleware (placed at the top)
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  console.log(`${req.method} ${req.path}`);
  next();
});

// ... (all previous routes here) ...

// 2. 404 Fallback for unknown routes
app.use((req, res) => {
  res.status(404).json({ error: "Route not found" });
});

// 3. Global Error Handler
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error("Unexpected Error:", err.message);
  res.status(500).json({ error: "An unexpected server error occurred" });
});

