12. Saving Tasks to a JSON File

Volatility of Memory: Variables (let tasks = []) exist in RAM. When the Node process stops or restarts, RAM is wiped.
node:fs/promises: Provides asynchronous methods (readFile, writeFile) to interact with the file system without blocking the main thread.
JSON.parse() / JSON.stringify(): Used to convert the text string from the file back into a JavaScript array, and vice versa.
Concurrent Writes: High traffic can trigger multiple file writes simultaneously, potentially corrupting the JSON file. A true database handles concurrency locks automatically.

// src/storage.js
import fs from 'node:fs/promises';

export async function saveTasks(filePath, tasks) {
  const data = JSON.stringify(tasks, null, 2);
  await fs.writeFile(filePath, data, 'utf8');
}

export async function loadTasks(filePath) {
  try {
    const data = await fs.readFile(filePath, 'utf8');
    return JSON.parse(data);
  } catch (err) {
    // Basic catch: handled properly in Step 14
    return [];
  }
}

