13. Environment Variables and Backend Configuration

Environment Variables: External configuration injected into the program by the OS, accessible via process.env.
All values are strings: Even PORT=3000 is parsed as the string "3000".
Separating config: Logic shouldn't be hardcoded. Port numbers and file paths change between development, testing, and production servers.
.env.example: Provides a template showing developers what variables the app requires, committed to Git without real passwords or keys.

# .env.example
PORT=3000
TASKS_FILE=./data/tasks.json

// src/server.js
import 'dotenv/config'; // Automatically loads .env file
import app from './app.js';

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
const TASKS_FILE = process.env.TASKS_FILE || './data/tasks.json';

app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server running on port ${PORT}`);
  console.log(`Using data file: ${TASKS_FILE}`);
});

