14. Handling Missing and Corrupted Data Files

Missing file (ENOENT): If fs.readFile looks for a file that hasn't been created yet, Node throws an ENOENT (Error No Entry). Returning an empty array [] is a safe, graceful recovery.
Invalid JSON: If the file exists but contains corrupted text, JSON.parse throws a SyntaxError.
Preserving Data: If corruption occurs, the server MUST crash or log an error instead of returning []. Returning [] would cause the app to overwrite the corrupted (but potentially recoverable) file with a blank array on the next save, resulting in catastrophic data loss.

// src/storage.js (enhanced loadTasks)
import fs from 'node:fs/promises';

export async function loadTasks(filePath) {
  try {
    const data = await fs.readFile(filePath, 'utf8');
    const parsed = JSON.parse(data);
    
    if (!Array.isArray(parsed)) {
      throw new Error("Corrupted data: JSON is not an array.");
    }
    return parsed;

  } catch (err) {
    if (err.code === 'ENOENT') {
      return []; // Safe to return empty array if file doesn't exist yet
    }
    // For JSON parsing errors or permissions issues, throw to prevent overwriting
    throw err; 
  }
}

